      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-143587",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin \u03b8 - 1$ for $\u03b8 \u2208 [0, 2\u03c0)$. Given that\n\n$x^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = \\sin (\u03b8) + \\cos (2\u03b8), y^{\u2032}(\u03b8) =-\\cos (\u03b8) + \\sin (2\u03b8)$,\nand $x^{\u2032}(\\frac{\u03c0}{2}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{\u2032}(\\frac{\u03c0}{2}) = 0$\n2. $lim_(\u03b8 \u2192 (\u03c0/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = \u221e$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\u03b8 = \\frac{\u03c0}{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n$\\cos (\u03b8) - 2\\sin (2\u03b8) \u2192 0^{-}as\u03b8 \u2192 (\\frac{\u03c0}{2})^{-}; \\cos (\u03b8) - 2\\sin (2\u03b8) \u2192 0^{+}as\u03b8 \u2192 (\\frac{\u03c0}{2})^{+}$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
