      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-145085",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the polar curve $r = \u03b8^{2}\\cos \u03b8$ for $\u03b8 \u2208 (- \u03c0, \u03c0)$. Given that\n\n$x^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = 2\u03b8\\cos^{2} (\u03b8) - \u03b8^{2}\\sin (2\u03b8), y^{\u2032}(\u03b8) = \u03b8\\sin (2\u03b8) + \u03b8^{2}\\cos (2\u03b8)$,\nand $x^{\u2032}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{\u2032}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(\u03b8 \u2192 0^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = \u221e$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\u03b8 = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
